{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cabin Climb-Rate Control.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cabin Climb-Rate Control.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cabin climb rate control cabin climb rate control","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cabin Climb-Rate Control.md","fileName":"Cabin Climb-Rate Control.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cabin Climb-Rate Control","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 21:32\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Aircraft Gyroscopes and Cabin Pressurization]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Aircraft Gyroscopes and Cabin Pressurization","flightscience.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cabin Climb-Rate Control.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cabin Climb-Rate Control.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cabin climb rate control cabin climb rate control","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cabin Climb-Rate Control.md","fileName":"Cabin Climb-Rate Control.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cabin Climb-Rate Control","heading":"Cabin Climb-Rate Control","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Cabin pressure is changed gradually so the cabin reaches its selected altitude near, but normally before, the aircraft reaches cruise or landing altitude. A rate that is too low can let pressure differential exceed its limit during a rapid aircraft climb, while an unnecessarily high rate increases occupant discomfort. Setting the rate therefore coordinates aircraft vertical speed, target cabin altitude, and a timing buffer.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Aircraft Gyroscopes and Cabin Pressurization","flightscience.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cabin Climb-Rate Control.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cabin Climb-Rate Control.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearch